# Mika — Environment Variables
# Place at ~/.mika/.env for automatic loading (CLI + server).
# For the gateway, place in the working directory.
# Shell-set MIKA_* env vars always take precedence over this file.

# ── Per-Provider API Keys ──
# Set the API key for your active provider (set llm_provider in config.toml).
# Only the active provider's key is required.

# Anthropic (default provider)
MIKA_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...

# OpenAI (also used for Layer 3 vector search embeddings)
# MIKA_OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...

# OpenRouter (access 200+ models via single key)
# MIKA_OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...

# Groq (ultra-fast inference)
# MIKA_GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_...

# Ollama (local, no key needed)
# MIKA_OLLAMA_API_KEY=

# Mistral
# MIKA_MISTRAL_API_KEY=...

# Google AI (Gemini)
# MIKA_GOOGLE_API_KEY=...

# DeepSeek
# MIKA_DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=...

# Z.AI (direct GLM API — bypasses OpenRouter margin for native GLM routing). See https://z.ai/docs/api
# MIKA_ZAI_API_KEY=...
# MIKA_ZAI_MODEL=glm-5.2

# Web search: NOT an agent key since mika#1971. The agent's `web_search` no
# longer reads a key of its own — it POSTs /internal/search on the gateway, and
# the key lives there. Setting MIKA_BRAVE_API_KEY here activates nothing.
# See "Search substrate" under Gateway mode below.

# ── Knowledge Graph LLM (optional) ──
# Shared fallback model for KG extraction and resolution. Format: provider/model.
# OpenRouter is recommended — Anthropic direct is typically ~10× more expensive
# for bulk NER and will trigger a WARN (kg_anthropic_provider) at startup.
# MIKA_KG_INGESTION_MODEL=openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v3
# Model for NER + fact-triple extraction (#690). Falls back to KG_INGESTION_MODEL.
# MIKA_KG_EXTRACTION_MODEL=openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v3
# Model for entity resolution disambiguation (#691). Falls back to KG_INGESTION_MODEL.
# Mid-tier model recommended for better judgment on ambiguous matches.
# MIKA_KG_RESOLUTION_MODEL=openrouter/anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5

# Per-batch LLM call cap on KG startup extraction and resolution (#757). Default: 500.
# Worst-case per-startup cost is 2 × N_agents × MIKA_KG_BATCH_BUDGET (extraction +
# resolution batches, one each per agent). Overflow emits a WARN (kg_budget_exhausted)
# and leaves remaining work for the next restart. `0` disables the phase entirely.
# MIKA_KG_BATCH_BUDGET=500

# Absolute path to the docs root for KG lexical ingestion (#738). Defaults to
# <CWD>/docs/solutions when unset — works in containers where the Dockerfile copies
# docs/ into the workdir. Needed on hosts where the service CWD != repo root
# (e.g., OpenRC supervise-daemon). Also settable as kg_docs_root in config.toml.
# MIKA_KG_DOCS_ROOT=/path/to/mika-repo/docs/solutions

# Colon-separated list of absolute paths for multi-corpus agents (e.g., mika-arch).
# Global fallback; per-agent [kg].docs_roots in identity.toml takes precedence.
# Linux/macOS only.
# MIKA_KG_DOCS_ROOTS=

# ── Provider Selection ──
# Set in config.toml (not here): llm_provider = "anthropic"
# Available: anthropic, openai, openrouter, groq, ollama, mistral, google, deepseek
#
# Per-provider model (set in config.toml):
#   anthropic_model = "claude-sonnet-4-6"    (default)
#   openai_model = "gpt-4o"                  (default)
#   openrouter_model = "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4"  (default)
#   groq_model = "llama-3.3-70b-versatile"   (default)
#   ollama_model = "llama3"                   (default)
#   mistral_model = "mistral-large-latest"    (default)
#   google_model = "gemini-2.5-flash"         (default)
#   deepseek_model = "deepseek-chat"          (default)

# Optional: Override defaults
# MIKA_LLM_MAX_TOKENS=4096
# MIKA_DB_PATH=mika.db
# MIKA_LOG_LEVEL=info
# MIKA_LOG_FORMAT=json                  # json (default) or pretty (human-readable, for local dev)
# MIKA_EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small
# MIKA_EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=512
# MIKA_DEV_MODE=false                # Auto-provision mika-dev + mika-qa agents on startup
# MIKA_DISABLE_BUNDLED_SKILLS=false  # WARNING: do not enable in production
# MIKA_DISABLE_AGENT_PROVISIONING=false  # Prevent dev_mode from overwriting agent files

# Optional: where this instance runs (mika#2290). `local` | `cloud`, case-insensitive.
# A POSED fact, never inferred — nothing in the process can observe its own hosting.
# Set it BEFORE first startup (service EnvironmentFile / K8s ConfigMap), like
# MIKA_AGENT_TIER: it is read once per process and cached, so setting it on a running
# process has no effect.
#
# ABSENCE IS NOT "local" — it resolves to Unknown, and the agent then says it cannot
# reliably determine where it runs rather than guessing. That is the deliberate inverse
# of MIKA_AGENT_TIER (whose absence means "operator workstation"): an unset deployment
# is exactly the state in which a cloud tenant told a guest "tout tourne en local, tes
# données ne quittent pas ta machine" on 2026-09-11. On a genuinely local install,
# declare it — one line — and the agent regains the right to say so.
# MIKA_DEPLOYMENT=local

# Optional: Task engine limits
# MIKA_MAX_AGENT_TASKS_PER_SESSION=25  # Max agent-created tasks per session (default: 25)
# MIKA_CALLBACK_WATCHDOG_GRACE_PERIOD_SECS=120  # Grace period after subprocess death before failing callback (default: 120)

# Optional: LLM timeout budgets (mika#1660, mika#2189)
# These two are ONE setting in two halves. The per-call plafond must fit STRICTLY
# inside the per-agent envelope: otherwise a single call can consume the whole turn
# and leave the agent loop no budget for a second step. Mika refuses to construct a
# provider under such a pair, naming both values. That check happens at provider
# construction, not at boot — a mika that starts is not proof that its budgets are
# valid; the first LLM call is.
# Both are also settable PER AGENT in ~/.mika/agents/<name>/config.toml as
# `llm_http_timeout_secs` and `agent_total_timeout_secs` — which is the granularity
# the problem has, since a fleet-wide var cannot give a slow reasoning agent a
# different geometry from a fast one.
# MIKA_LLM_HTTP_TIMEOUT_SECS=120  # Per-call plafond: per-request HTTP timeout for
                                  # non-Anthropic LLM providers (openai-compatible +
                                  # ollama). Default 120. Values <10 are rejected at
                                  # provider construction. (mika#1660)
# MIKA_AGENT_TOTAL_TIMEOUT_SECS=300  # Per-agent turn envelope. Default 300 (unchanged
                                     # — mika#2189 made it settable, it did not move the
                                     # fleet default). 0 or unparseable falls back to the
                                     # default with a WARN: an envelope of zero would abort
                                     # every turn before its first step.

# Optional: Runtime observability (LLM call + tool call recording)
# MIKA_STORE_LLM_CALLS=true       # Store LLM call metadata in SQLite (default: true)
# MIKA_STORE_TOOL_CALLS=true      # Store full tool call I/O in SQLite (default: true)
# MIKA_LOG_LLM_BODIES=false       # Log full LLM request/response bodies (default: false, dev-only)

# Optional: claude-pilot subprocess transcript capture (mika#1705)
# Captures the implementation-reasoning corpus (LLM calls inside claude-pilot
# dispatches) that the in-process llm_calls table never sees. When enabled, the
# executor injects ANTHROPIC_LOG_FILE for dev-pilot/dev-groom subprocesses;
# claude-pilot-py appends JSONL per LLM call; the engine tick ingests finished
# files into the pilot_transcripts table and deletes the JSONL.
# MIKA_LOG_PILOT_TRANSCRIPTS=true          # Capture pilot transcripts (default: true, gateable)
# MIKA_PILOT_TRANSCRIPT_RETENTION_DAYS=90  # Delete transcript rows older than N days (default: 90)

# Optional: OpenTelemetry trace export (requires --features telemetry at build time)
# NOTE: The endpoint must include the full path with /v1/traces — it is NOT auto-appended.
# MIKA_TELEMETRY_ENABLED=true
# MIKA_OTLP_ENDPOINT=https://cloud.langfuse.com/api/public/otel/v1/traces  (Langfuse)
# MIKA_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318/v1/traces                       (Jaeger)
# MIKA_OTLP_AUTH_HEADER=publicKey:secretKey  (raw or base64-encoded, Langfuse only)

# Optional: GitHub token for agent operations (context injection, work item enrichment, PR merge).
# Needs Pull requests R/W, Issues R/W, Contents R scopes.
# MIKA_GITHUB_TOKEN=ghp_...

# GH_TOKEN — DO NOT set in ~/.mika/.env. This var is for host-level gh CLI
# identity (e.g., Claude Code / claude-pilot sessions). Mika agent operations
# use MIKA_GITHUB_TOKEN instead, which run_gh injects as GH_TOKEN automatically.
# Setting GH_TOKEN in .mika/.env collapses both identities into one.

# -- GitHub App (optional, preferred over PAT for agent operations) --
# When configured, installation tokens replace MIKA_GITHUB_TOKEN for run_gh,
# context injection, and work item enrichment. Falls back to PAT if exchange fails.
# Register at: https://github.com/settings/apps
# Per-agent: set these in ~/.mika/agents/<name>/.env for per-agent App identity.
# MIKA_GITHUB_APP_ID=123456
# MIKA_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=<base64 -w0 < your-app.pem>
# MIKA_GITHUB_APP_INSTALLATION_ID=78901234
# MIKA_GITHUB_APP_LOGIN=mika-dev[bot]  # Bot login for assignee filtering (optional)

# Optional: Investigation panel GitHub token + repo for dashboard issue creation.
# Used ONLY by the investigation panel — not for agent operations.
# Token needs `repo` scope (private repos) or `public_repo` scope (public repos).
# MIKA_INVESTIGATE_GITHUB_TOKEN=ghp_...
# MIKA_GITHUB_REPO=owner/repo

# Optional: Dashboard CORS origin (default: http://localhost:5173)
# MIKA_CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# Optional: mika-spirit URL for CLI dashboard commands (default: http://localhost:8080)
# Used by `mika dashboard start/stop/status` to reach the running server.
# MIKA_SPIRIT_URL=http://localhost:8080

# Optional: mika-gateway URL for CLI webhook DLQ commands (default: http://localhost:3001)
# Used by `mika webhook list-dead/replay/replay-all` to reach the running gateway.
# MIKA_GATEWAY_URL=http://localhost:3001

# Optional: Enable embedded dashboard SPA at /dashboard/ (default: false)
# When enabled, the pre-built React dashboard is served from the binary.
# Can be toggled at runtime via `mika dashboard start/stop` or the API.
# MIKA_DASHBOARD_ENABLED=true

# Optional: Separate bearer token for read-only dashboard API routes (/api/v1/*).
# If unset, dashboard routes accept MIKA_INTERNAL_TOKEN (backwards compatible).
# Use this to give dashboard users a token that cannot mutate agent state.
# MIKA_DASHBOARD_TOKEN=<64 hex chars — openssl rand -hex 32>

# Optional: Log file paths for server/gateway (logs go to stdout + file when set)
# MIKA_SPIRIT_LOG_FILE=/var/log/mika/server.log

# --- Gateway mode (mika-gateway binary) ---
# Required when running the gateway:
# MIKA_DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@localhost/mika
# MIKA_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=<your Telegram bot token>
# MIKA_TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=<64 hex chars — openssl rand -hex 32>
# MIKA_TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_URL=https://your-domain.com/webhook/telegram
# MIKA_GATEWAY_PORT=8080
# MIKA_AGENT_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080  # local E2E testing only
# MIKA_GATEWAY_LOG_FILE=                     # optional log file path

# --- Search substrate (mika#1807 / mika#1971 / mika#2407) ---
# Since mika#1971 every agent's `web_search` goes through the gateway:
# POST /internal/search. These two keys are what activate it, and BOTH are
# needed — a key on its own activates nothing. Without the selector the endpoint
# answers 404 search_upstream_not_configured and every tenant silently loses web
# search while /readyz stays green. That is mika#2407: on 2026-09-18 an image
# rotation left the gateway without either, and six tenants were mute for ~20 h.
# MIKA_SEARCH_UPSTREAM=brave
# MIKA_BRAVE_API_KEY=BSA...           # required when MIKA_SEARCH_UPSTREAM=brave
#
# Declare that this deployment EXPECTS search, and losing it stops the gateway
# from starting (so the rollout fails) instead of serving mute tenants. Absent or
# empty means not required — the legitimate shape of a dev workstation and of any
# deployment that never wanted search. An unrecognized value is treated as
# REQUIRED, with a WARN naming it: a rollout refused on a typo is repaired in a
# minute, mute tenants only once a human notices.
# MIKA_SEARCH_REQUIRED=1
#
# After a rotation, verify from the outside — one real search, three verdicts
# (0 healthy / 1 substrate broken / 2 nothing verified):
#   MIKA_INTERNAL_TOKEN=<token> scripts/smoke-search-substrate https://<gateway>

# --- Telegram outbound rendering kill-switch (mika#2291, optional) ---
# Default: ARMED. Outbound Telegram text is rendered with parse_mode=HTML, so the
# markdown an agent writes (**bold**, `code`, [label](url)) arrives rendered
# instead of raw. On a 400 while parse_mode was set, the gateway re-sends ONCE
# without parse_mode using the plain text — so the worst case is exactly the
# pre-mika#2291 behaviour minus the raw markers, and no message can be lost to a
# rendering.
# Set to 0 / false / off / no to disarm (plain text, markers stripped). Absent,
# empty, or any unrecognized value stays ARMED and logs a WARN naming the value —
# a typo must not silently switch the rendering off.
# Read ONCE at startup, not hot-swappable: set it here / in the ConfigMap BEFORE
# the gateway starts.
# MIKA_TELEGRAM_HTML_RENDER=0

# --- mika-manager Phase 1 (LECTURE SEULE, optional) ---
# Milestone-scope operational coordinator. Reads milestone state via `gh`,
# assesses it, and POSTs a Markdown report — or writes it to an offline sink.
# Zero write authority: no dispatch, no label mutation, no PR merge.
#
# THE FEATURE GATE. Unset → the cadence loop never spawns. `<owner/repo>#<n>`.
# MIKA_MANAGER_TARGET_MILESTONE=senara-solutions/mika#1799
#
# Cadence. Heartbeat is the 6h plancher ("l'absence d'event EST l'event"); the
# poll interval is how often state change is checked and is clamped to
# min(poll, heartbeat). Both are three-tier: absent → default; unparseable or
# zero → default with a WARN.
# MIKA_MANAGER_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_SECS=21600
# MIKA_MANAGER_POLL_INTERVAL_SECS=300
# MIKA_MANAGER_SILENCE_THRESHOLD_DAYS=3
#
# Delivery. Normal severities go to DELIVERY_URL, Severity::Blocked goes to
# ESCALATION_URL (a deliberately distinct route — a blocked report must not
# queue behind the normal relay). An URL that is unset or empty is NOT an
# error: the report goes to the offline sink instead. Which route is actually
# in force is stated once at startup by the `manager_delivery_resolved` log
# line (mika#2267) — read it rather than inferring from these comments.
# MIKA_MANAGER_DELIVERY_URL=https://cm.example.com/api/v1/messages/dispatch
# MIKA_MANAGER_DELIVERY_TOKEN=<bearer token for the delivery endpoint>
# MIKA_MANAGER_ESCALATION_URL=https://cm.example.com/api/v1/escalate
#
# Optional cm executor-liveness probe. Unset → the liveness field stays None.
# MIKA_MANAGER_HEALTH_URL=https://cm.example.com/api/v1/agents/mika-dev/health
#
# State directories. Defaults are $HOME/.mika/manager/{checkpoints,sink}, with
# /tmp/mika-manager as a last-resort root when HOME is unset too.
#
# THE SINK IS NOT A DEAD END SINCE mika#2267: read it with
#   mika milestone reports            # list, most recent first
#   mika milestone reports --latest   # print the most recent report
# Before that subcommand existed nothing read this directory, so "the channel
# is broken" and "the channel is a well" produced the same bytes for a human
# reader: none. The listing always names the directory it consulted, because
# the CLI (operator) and the daemon (service, possibly another HOME) can
# resolve different ones.
# MIKA_MANAGER_CHECKPOINT_DIR=/var/lib/mika-manager/checkpoints
# MIKA_MANAGER_OFFLINE_SINK_DIR=/var/lib/mika-manager/sink

# --- Alerte « un build vert, un verdict qui n'arrive pas » (mika#2515) ---
# Quand un `build_mika` réussit, le tour de callback qui doit poster le verdict
# QA peut ne jamais tourner (verrou d'agent pris — contention) ou échouer. Le
# filet moteur mika#2368/#2515 ne couvre que les tours qui ONT tourné : pour les
# deux autres populations, l'AC demande « SOIT un verdict posté SOIT une alerte
# nommée », et c'est cette alerte.
#
# Elle NE POSTE RIEN, délibérément : sous contention le verdict n'est pas perdu,
# il est en file, et poser un `hold[review]` sur une PR dont le vrai verdict
# arrive dans dix minutes serait une affirmation trompeuse — et sortirait la PR
# de la population du réconciliateur mika#2334 pour de bon.
#
# Lire :
#   grep qa_build_verdict_undelivered "$MIKA_SPIRIT_LOG_FILE" \
#     | jq -c '{task_id, target, cause, age_secs, deferrals, attempts}'
# `cause` ∈ {agent_busy_starvation, turn_failed, quarantined, never_attempted}.
#
# Kill-switch, défaut ARMÉ. `0`/`false`/`off`/`no` désarment sans redéploiement ;
# une valeur non reconnue est dite et laisse armé. Désarmé, une ligne INFO
# `qa_build_verdict_alert_disabled` le dit au démarrage — sans quoi un
# instrument désarmé se lirait exactement comme un instrument sain.
# MIKA_QA_BUILD_VERDICT_ALERT=0
#
# Fenêtre avant alerte, en secondes (défaut 540). Arithmétique, pas une
# rondeur : l'AC demande « dans les 10 minutes » et le balayage tourne à 60 s,
# donc 540 + 60 = 600 s est la borne haute effective. Le nombre est posé contre
# l'AC, PAS mesuré — la sous-population « callback de build dont un tour QA
# attend le retour » ne l'a jamais été. HALTE : si la distribution montre du
# trafic nominal, c'est ce seuil qui monte, jamais l'alerte qu'on désarme.
# MIKA_QA_BUILD_VERDICT_ALERT_AGE_SECS=540
#
# Profondeur du balayage, en jours (défaut 7 — la même valeur que
# `dispatch_undelivered_callbacks`, pour que les deux bras voient la MÊME
# population).
# MIKA_QA_BUILD_VERDICT_ALERT_LOOKBACK_DAYS=7

# --- GitHub webhook (optional, enables POST /webhook/github) ---
# MIKA_GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=<your GitHub App webhook secret — arbitrary string>
# MIKA_GITHUB_APP_ID=<GitHub App ID — for bot self-event filtering>

# --- Orchestrator inbox v2 (mika#1189, optional) ---
# When `1` or `true`, exposes:
#   POST /orchestrator/inbox/{orchestrator_id}/message   (bearer auth, 256KB)
#   GET  /orchestrator/inbox/{orchestrator_id}/stream    (bearer auth, SSE)
# Otherwise both endpoints return 404 and the hourly retention task is not
# spawned. Dual-write with mika-platform#100 filesystem inbox during migration.
# `2` (gateway-only cutover) is reserved for a future ticket and currently
# treated as disabled.
# MIKA_ORCHESTRATOR_INBOX_ENABLED=1
